After reflecting on our approach to finding the function $g(x)$, it becomes clear that a useful strategy is to simplify the expressions by multiplying and dividing by an appropriate factor. \\We can rewrite the given equation as: \[(x^{2^{2008}-1}-1)g(x) = (x^{2^{2008}}-1)(x^{2^{2007}}-1)(x^{2^{2006}}-1)\cdots (x^{2^2}-1)(x+1)-1\]
\\Now, we utilize the fact that $x^{2^n}-1 = (x^{2^{n-1}}-1)(x^{2^{n-1}}+1)$. On substitution, this yields: \[(x^{2^{2008}-1}-1)g(x) = (x^{2^{2008}}-1)(x^{2^{2007}}-1)(x^{2^{2006}}-1)\cdots (x^{2^2}-1)(x+1)-1\] \[(x+1)\underbrace{(x^{2^{2007}}-1)}_a\underbrace{(x^{2^{2006}}-1)}_b\cdots\underbrace{(x^{2^2}-1)}_c(x^{2^3} - 1)(x^{2^3} + 1) \cdots (x^{2^5}) + 1 computable\]
\\On observation, all numbers $(x^{2^i} + 1)$ cancel out pairwise and we ultimately reach: \begin{align*}\underbrace{(x+1)}_{k}g(x) = (x^{2^{2008}}-1)\end{align*}
\\Ignoring brackets: \begin{align*}g(x) = \frac{x^{2^{2008}}-1}{x+1}\end{align*}
\\ Hence, for $x=2$: \begin{align*}g(x) = \frac{2^{2^{2008}}-1}{2+1}= \frac{2^{2^{2008}}-1}{3}\end{align*}
\\Answer: <answer> \boxed{ \frac{2^{2^{2008}}-1}{3} } </answer>

